    <name>
      <text>MAT322 - Log Integral Simpson</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Approximate the integral \(\int_{1}^{@b@} x^2 \ln(x) \, dx\) using the Composite Simpson's Rule with \(n = 4\) subintervals.</p>
<p>Give your answer to at least 5 decimal places.</p>
<p>Answer: [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>We have \(a=1, b={@b@}, n=4\). The step size is \(h = \frac{{@b@}-1}{4} = {@h@}\).</p>
<p>Nodes: \(x_0=1, x_1={@1+h@}, x_2={@1+2*h@}, x_3={@1+3*h@}, x_4={@b@}\).</p>
<p>Simpson's Rule:</p>
<p>\[ S_4 = \frac{h}{3} [ f(x_0) + 4f(x_1) + 2f(x_2) + 4f(x_3) + f(x_4) ] \]</p>
<p>\[ S_4 \approx {@ta1@} \]</p>]]></text>
